    id: "77bd21b22feb5141ef6b",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "France rebukes world over Gaza as Canada, UK back Palestinian state at UNGA",
    summary: "France's Macron says suffering in Gaza should 'shame all of us' as Canada's Carney pledges $71m for two-state efforts.",
    preview: "France's Macron says the suffering in Gaza should 'shame all of us' as Canada's Carney pledges $71m for two-state efforts. UK Prime Minister vows not to ‘stand by’ as Palestinians suffer France, Canada and the United Kingdom have expressed support for a Palestinian state at the United Nations General Assembly (UNGA), with French President Emmanuel Macron condemning Israel’s restrictions on aid to Gaza as a “shame” for the world.",
    body: "France's Macron says the suffering in Gaza should 'shame all of us' as Canada's Carney pledges $71m for two-state efforts.\n\nUK Prime Minister vows not to ‘stand by’ as Palestinians suffer\n\nFrance, Canada and the United Kingdom have expressed support for a Palestinian state at the United Nations General Assembly (UNGA), with French President Emmanuel Macron condemning Israel’s restrictions on aid to Gaza as a “shame” for the world.\n\nMacron’s impassioned address on Tuesday came as Israeli forces killed at least three Palestinians in Gaza City, despite a “ceasefire” brokered by the United States in October. More than 73,900 people have been killed in Gaza since Israel’s genocidal war began in October 2023, including 1,399 since the “ceasefire” took effect.\n\nRecommended Stories\n\nlist of 3 items\n\n- list 1 of 3Canadians warm to EU ‘associate membership’ proposal\n\n- list 2 of 3Trump tariffs hit Canada’s dairy farmers as US sales stall\n\n- list 3 of 3France’s Macron and Canada’s Carney announce plans to build stronger ties\n\nIn his speech to the UNGA, Macron noted that France had been among a dozen countries that recognised a Palestinian state a year ago. But he asked, “What credibility do we have if we continue to remain inactive in the face of Gaza?”\n\n“Some boast of a supposed peace, but this peace hasn’t reopened humanitarian routes for a single second,” he said.\n\n“Must we stand by and watch this spectacle that shames us all? Never!”\n\nMacron also criticised what he called Israel’s daily “violations” in the occupied West Bank.\n\n“All those who claim that Israel’s security depends on violating the sovereignty of all its neighbours are making a fatal mistake, weakening Israel’s security for today and tomorrow, and threatening it,” he warned.\n\nMacron, along with UK Prime Minister Andy Burnham, Canadian Prime Minister Mike Carney and Saudi Foreign Minister Prince Faisal bin Farhan Al Saud, also chaired a meeting at the UNGA on Palestinian statehood.\n\nBurnham told delegates from dozens of countries that failure to deliver a two-state solution will “corrode trust” in politicians who have talked about the issue but not taken action.\n\n“We all live in an era of low trust in politics. Many people in this room, many politicians around the world, have spoken for decades about a two-state solution,” he said. “But if we fail to back those words with deeds, we will further corrode trust in the political, diplomatic and international process.”\n\nBurnham added the UK has also banned trade with illegal Israeli settlements in the West Bank, describing Israel’s E1 settlement project as “a deliberate attempt to block a two-state solution”. The project would bisect the occupied West Bank and cut it off from East Jerusalem, fragmenting the land Palestinians hope will form the heart of a future state.\n\nCarney meanwhile announced that Canada would give 100 million Canadian dollars ($71m) in aid to Palestinians to preserve “the possibility of a two-state solution”.\n\nHe said 80 percent of the funding will go towards direct humanitarian aid, while the remaining 20 percent will be allocated to “support peace and security”, including Canada’s multiyear initiative to train Palestinian police in the occupied West Bank.\n\nCarney said Palestinians in Gaza continued to endure “immense human suffering”, while Israelis faced “existential threats from Hamas and from Iran and its proxies”. He also criticised Israel’s E1 settlement project and said Canada intended to introduce restrictions on goods produced in settlements in the occupied territory.\n\nCanada is among 12 countries that have announced such trade restrictions.\n\nSince the beginning of Israel’s genocidal war on Gaza, Ottawa has pledged more than 600 million Canadian dollars ($426m) in aid for Palestinian civilians.",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "Al Jazeera",
    url: "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/9/23/canada-pledges-aid-for-palestine-as-uk-and-france-demand-action-at-unga?traffic_source=rss",
